{
 "_note": "THE IMPERATIVE SURFACE, as pure target resolution: `dsx.scroll(ref).to({x,y,animated})` / `.toTop()` / `.toBottom()` / `.toElement({ref,align,animated})` reduce to ONE offset, and this corpus pins that offset. The ref registry and the animation are renderer plumbing; the arithmetic is not, and it is where the bugs are. THREE RULES. An imperative call NEVER over-scrolls: every target clamps to 0..max, so `to({y: 99999})` parks on the rail instead of leaving a band of nothing under the content, and a negative target lands on the origin. `toTop`/`toBottom` name the PRIMARY AXIS, not the vertical one, so on `axis=\"horizontal\"` they mean start and end — the familiar words keep working on a rail. `align=\"nearest\"` is the only alignment that may decide NOT to move: a child already fully visible stays where it is, which is what makes `toElement` safe to call on every selection change. A child taller than the viewport resolves to its start under `nearest`. AN UNREALISED ROW IN A VIRTUALISED LIST REFUSES (null) rather than guessing at an offset it cannot know; a virtualiser that carries an estimate passes the estimated frame and gets a real answer, then re-resolves once the row is realised. Cases are {command, geometry, axis} -> the resolved offset, or null for a refusal.",
 "cases": [
  {
   "name": "to({y}) moves the vertical plane and leaves x alone",
   "command": {
    "kind": "to",
    "y": 900
   },
   "geometry": {
    "x": 0,
    "y": 600,
    "viewportWidth": 390,
    "viewportHeight": 800,
    "contentWidth": 390,
    "contentHeight": 2000
   },
   "axis": "vertical",
   "expect": {
    "x": 0.0,
    "y": 900.0,
    "animated": true
   }
  },
  {
   "name": "to() clamps past the end rather than leaving a gap under the content",
